{"question_id": "ac-003", "category": "authority_conflict", "question": "Is there verified evidence that LuminaSpark 2.0 relied on HIPAA-protected medical charts?", "expected_answer_keywords": ["no", "not", "rumor", "unverified"], "forbidden_keywords": ["medium-high", "leak confirmed", "40 million"], "expected_doc_keys": ["lumina-spark-2-0-release", "lumina-spark-2-0-rumor"], "expected_abstain": false, "notes": "Should prefer primary denial over rumor.", "answer": "No, there is no verified evidence that LuminaSpark 2.0 relied on HIPAA-protected medical charts. According to primary-source documentation from Lumina AI Labs, the company published a dataset consent audit attestation (DSA-2026-03) that underwent third-party verification, explicitly confirming that no HIPAA-regulated or scraped patient data entered the model's pretraining pipelines [C3]. While unverified rumors have circulated regarding the use of scraped medical records [C4], these claims lack supporting public filings or regulatory evidence linking the developer to healthcare entities for protected health information usage.", "abstained": false, "cited_doc_keys": ["lumina-spark-2-0-release", "lumina-spark-2-0-rumor"], "retrieved_doc_keys": ["lumina-spark-0-9-deprecation", "lumina-spark-1-5-release", "lumina-spark-2-0-release", "lumina-spark-2-0-rumor"], "latency_s": 27.989854238927364, "tokens_used": 2843, "answer_correctness": 0.75, "forbidden_violation": false, "evidence_recall_at_k": 1.0, "citation_precision": 1.0, "citation_faithfulness": true, "temporal_correctness": null, "supersession_correctness": null, "abstention_accuracy": true}
{"question_id": "th-001", "category": "thesis", "question": "What evidence supports the thesis that sub-10B model inference costs are collapsing?", "expected_answer_keywords": ["0.85", "4.2x", "0.68"], "forbidden_keywords": [], "expected_doc_keys": ["lumina-edge-quant-research", "lumina-api-pricing-feb-2026", "lumina-orion-edge-announcement"], "expected_abstain": false, "notes": "Thesis spans research, pricing, hardware.", "answer": "Evidence supporting the thesis that small-model inference costs are collapsing centers on rapid pricing reductions, hardware acceleration, and architectural shifts toward quantization. Key data points include:\n- API pricing was slashed from $2.40 to $0.85 per million output tokens between November 2025 and February 2026, with a stated corporate target to cut unit costs an additional 20% by Q4 2026 [C1][C6].\n- The January 2026 general availability of the Orion Edge NPU enabled on-premise inference for LuminaSpark 1.5 at just $0.68 per million tokens [C7].\n- The platform has structurally lowered baseline compute requirements by defaulting new API keys to quantized serving pools rather than fp16, charging premiums only for explicit fp16 routing [C4], alongside the deployment of quantized Orion Edge bundles for newer releases like LuminaSpark 2.0 [C5].\n\nThese technical and commercial developments directly underpin the leadership's strategic thesis that collapsing inference costs will drive agentic adoption, with projections that edge inference margin pools for sub-12B models (which encompass the sub-10B range) will surpass cloud training margins by 2027 [C8].", "abstained": false, "cited_doc_keys": ["lumina-api-pricing-feb-2026", "lumina-inference-funding", "lumina-small-model-thesis-news", "lumina-spark-2-0-release"], "retrieved_doc_keys": ["lumina-api-pricing-feb-2026", "lumina-inference-funding", "lumina-small-model-thesis-news", "lumina-spark-0-9-deprecation", "lumina-spark-2-0-release"], "latency_s": 33.09207477234304, "tokens_use